 "literal": "Forest crow's dark wing resembling / mayilai-garlanded, great-shouldered young girl / 'Of what class is she, this girl?' thus with trembling excitement / with me asks, victorious-speared great lord! / Lakshmi-desirable fitting quality's her beauty / to warriors except, to others will not become. / Green-legged heron's wide-open-mouthed chick / soft mud close-by bank having foraged and eaten, after that, / āral fish laid mustard-like eggs / sharp fine prawn's chick-with obtains / his wet-field lord, her father. And kings / because of not obtaining, great battle because of waging, / abundant corpses piled-high stacks, wounded elephants as oxen, / with swords fittingly every day threshing / greatness-possessing they, this girl's brothers indeed!",
 "translation": "The great lord with a victorious spear asks me in trembling excitement, 'Who is this young girl with beautiful shoulders and a garland of mayilai flowers, whose hair resembles the dark wings of a forest crow?' Her beauty, worthy of Lakshmi's desire, belongs only to fierce warriors and to no one else. Her father is the lord of fertile wet fields where the wide-mouthed chick of a green-legged heron, after foraging on the soft muddy bank, finds the mustard-like eggs laid by the āral fish along with the young of sharp-nosed prawns. Because the kings could not obtain her, they wage a great battle. In that field, her glorious brothers use swords to thresh daily, treating the high piles of corpses as stacks of grain and the fallen elephants as the threshing oxen!",
